# An interior wall block: one tile square, two units tall, flat topped.
#
# Y is up, +Z is north, one world unit is one map tile. The lowest vertex sits
# on y = 0 and the model is centred on x = 0 and z = 0, so the game places it
# by its feet.
#
# Faces are wound so that (c - a) x (b - a) points out of the solid, which is
# what tools/obj2cpp.py bakes as the face normal. Get it backwards and the
# model still draws, it just goes flat ambient dark, and that is exactly what
# the first version of this file did: a room with a black stripe along its
# near wall and nothing on screen to say why.
#
# Four faces, 8 triangles. No bottom and no north face: the camera stands
# south of the player and looks north, so the north side of a block is never
# the side facing anyone.
#
# Two units, not one. A one unit wall seen from three tiles away through this
# lens is four pixels of trim around a floor floating in black, and the room
# reads as a platform rather than as somewhere with a roof over it.
#
# Slightly over one tile wide on purpose. A run of these is a wall, and a
# hairline of floor showing between two blocks reads as a gap in it.

mtllib wall.mtl

v -0.52 0.00 -0.52
v  0.52 0.00 -0.52
v  0.52 0.00  0.52
v -0.52 0.00  0.52
v -0.52 2.00 -0.52
v  0.52 2.00 -0.52
v  0.52 2.00  0.52
v -0.52 2.00  0.52

usemtl wall_top
f 5 6 7 8

usemtl wall_face
f 1 2 6 5

usemtl wall_side
f 2 3 7 6
f 4 1 5 8
